WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
Can one family observation eliminate an inheritance model?

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.400
Watch this pedigree become a test instead of a pattern to memorize.

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.020
The answer depends on what each model says should be possible.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.880
This is why a pedigree works like a model test.

5
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.530
A pedigree records people, relationships, and a named phenotype.

6
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:32.510
Shapes identify family members, connecting lines show relationships, and fill marks

7
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.750
affected people. Always read its key.

8
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.130
The same symbol can mean something different when the chart defines it differently.

9
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:44.273
Now place a model over the chart.

10
00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:50.293
Each inheritance model predicts where an allele can travel and which relatives can

11
00:00:50.293 --> 00:00:55.593
receive it. The chart becomes evidence because every line now makes a claim.

12
00:00:56.683 --> 00:01:00.503
An autosomal route can move through either parent and either sex.

13
00:01:01.823 --> 00:01:07.263
A dominant model lets an affected heterozygous parent pass the allele to children of

14
00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:13.523
either sex. An affected child of two unaffected parents contradicts that simple

15
00:01:13.523 --> 00:01:14.523
dominant model.

16
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:18.597
A recessive allele can hide in unaffected carriers.

17
00:01:20.037 --> 00:01:24.277
It may appear among siblings when each parent contributes a recessive allele.

18
00:01:25.637 --> 00:01:29.577
Here, unaffected parents can carry the allele without showing the trait.

19
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.820
Sex-linked models restrict the route.

20
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.600
A father gives his X chromosome to daughters and his Y chromosome to sons.

21
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:44.400
That route makes sex and parental origin informative, not merely decorative.

22
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:48.633
An affected father also has an affected son.

23
00:01:49.973 --> 00:01:54.253
Could an X-linked allele have travelled directly from that father to that son?

24
00:01:55.513 --> 00:01:57.893
Follow the chromosome route before you answer.

25
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:08.440
No. The son's X chromosome came from his mother.

26
00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:14.960
Demonstrated father-to-son transmission therefore contradicts both X-linked models

27
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.080
under these assumptions.

28
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.480
An affected pair alone shows it only if the mother carries no copy.

29
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:25.803
Mitochondrial inheritance draws a different route.

30
00:02:27.163 --> 00:02:32.583
Mothers can transmit mitochondrial DNA to children, while fathers normally transmit

31
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:38.683
none. This maternal route differs from mitochondrial diseases caused by nuclear

32
00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:39.683
genes.

33
00:02:40.283 --> 00:02:44.783
Fitting predictions support a model; one contradiction can rule it out.

34
00:02:46.543 --> 00:02:51.003
Compatibility adds support, but an observation the model forbids carries sharper

35
00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:56.003
information. The model earns confidence by surviving such tests.

36
00:02:57.103 --> 00:02:59.843
That is the guiding rule for this simple-model test.

37
00:03:00.917 --> 00:03:02.397
That test has a scope.

38
00:03:03.597 --> 00:03:09.177
It assumes full penetrance, no new mutation, correct relationships, and the simple

39
00:03:09.177 --> 00:03:10.297
model being tested.

40
00:03:11.717 --> 00:03:15.957
Change an assumption, and a seeming contradiction may need another explanation.

41
00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:20.407
A fitting chart supports a model without proving it.

42
00:03:21.547 --> 00:03:25.507
Other models or hidden biology may predict the same small family.

43
00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:30.887
Small families often lack the relative who would distinguish those models.
